WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:03.000
-Welcome back to the show.
You look great.

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:04.133
I always love having you here.

00:00:04.133 --> 00:00:05.734
I really want to talk to you
about this.

00:00:05.734 --> 00:00:07.934
Congratulations, by the way,
because you graduated Harvard.

00:00:07.934 --> 00:00:09.767
-Yeah!
-This is a big deal.

00:00:09.767 --> 00:00:12.667
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:12.667 --> 00:00:15.100
-You came on this show
when you first got into Harvard.

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:16.834
-Yeah, four years ago.

00:00:16.834 --> 00:00:18.634
-And you did it on time.
-I did.

00:00:18.634 --> 00:00:20.968
-You crushed it, buddy! Yeah!
-Thank you.

00:00:20.968 --> 00:00:22.367
-Here you are on graduation day.

00:00:22.367 --> 00:00:26.734
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:26.734 --> 00:00:28.601
And here's your --
here's your mom,

00:00:28.601 --> 00:00:30.467
very proud mom, on graduation.

00:00:30.467 --> 00:00:33.267
-I love it.
-Yeah.

00:00:33.267 --> 00:00:35.300
I'm so happy for you.
I'm so proud of you.

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:37.000
-Thank you.
-What was your major?

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:39.234
What did you major in?
-Ooh. This is my favorite part.

00:00:39.234 --> 00:00:41.300
'Cause I get to say, "Oh, my
God, I graduated from Harvard,"

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:44.234
but I get to follow it up with,
"In social studies."

00:00:44.234 --> 00:00:47.234
Which -- Just a bruised ego
because of it.

00:00:47.234 --> 00:00:49.133
-Yeah, because
it just sounds like --

00:00:49.133 --> 00:00:50.968
-It sounds like
a fifth-grade class.

00:00:50.968 --> 00:00:53.901
-Yeah, it does actually. Yeah.
-It sounds like when we learned

00:00:53.901 --> 00:00:56.067
all of our basic facts...
-That's it, yeah.

00:00:56.067 --> 00:00:57.200
-...was social studies.

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:59.200
-I loved social studies, yeah.
-Yeah.

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:01.968
-But now you have -- you have
free time on your hands.

00:01:01.968 --> 00:01:04.601
I don't know how you did both,
because you were working

00:01:04.601 --> 00:01:06.367
on "Grown-ish"
as you were doing this.

00:01:06.367 --> 00:01:08.667
-Yeah, because --
I mean, the funny thing was,

00:01:08.667 --> 00:01:10.701
I took a gap year when
"Grown-ish" season one happened.

00:01:10.701 --> 00:01:12.133
-Yep.
a-And you know how shows work.

00:01:12.133 --> 00:01:13.934
I've been to shows where
we've pulled up episode seven,

00:01:13.934 --> 00:01:15.634
and they're like, "They didn't
tell you? It's canceled."

00:01:15.634 --> 00:01:17.100
So, suddenly we get season two,

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:19.667
and I was planning
to go to school.

00:01:19.667 --> 00:01:22.267
And I didn't know for sure if I
wanted to take another year off.

00:01:22.267 --> 00:01:24.634
I get to campus. And I was
in a Cornell West class.

00:01:24.634 --> 00:01:26.434
I was like, "Oh, my goodness.
I have to be here right now."

00:01:26.434 --> 00:01:28.701
So, then my manager had the
lovely job of calling Disney

00:01:28.701 --> 00:01:31.267
to be like, "So, she's
full-time enrolled in school."

00:01:31.267 --> 00:01:33.000
-Oh, my gosh.
-But luckily, I was surrounded

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:34.801
by a lot of supportive --
-How did you do both?

00:01:34.801 --> 00:01:36.267
You just flew back and forth?

00:01:36.267 --> 00:01:38.634
-I'd take a suitcase
to my Wednesday class...

00:01:38.634 --> 00:01:40.767
fly, work Thursday, Friday.

00:01:40.767 --> 00:01:43.000
and then fly back
Saturday or Sunday.

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:46.734
-Wow. That's so wild, because
I was talking to you backstage,

00:01:46.734 --> 00:01:49.501
and you were like, "Yeah, so,
all these events and things..."

00:01:49.501 --> 00:01:51.367
Like, I would see you
at the Met Gala,

00:01:51.367 --> 00:01:53.033
and while you were there,
you're thinking,

00:01:53.033 --> 00:01:55.534
"I have an essay due tomorrow."
-Oh.

00:01:55.534 --> 00:01:57.901
The amount of mental free space
I have --

00:01:57.901 --> 00:01:59.801
I think I'm slightly incoherent

00:01:59.801 --> 00:02:01.434
because there's too much
brain space now.

00:02:01.434 --> 00:02:02.801
-You're like,
"This is fantastic."

00:02:02.801 --> 00:02:04.367
-I have all thoughts firing
at all times.

00:02:04.367 --> 00:02:05.968
But, yeah,
I used to be at the Met like,

00:02:05.968 --> 00:02:07.968
"My paper on the
Frankfurt School is due,

00:02:07.968 --> 00:02:10.834
and I only got through 50%
of the readings before this."

00:02:10.834 --> 00:02:12.701
-Are you good with free time?
Are you --

00:02:12.701 --> 00:02:14.200
-I'm so bad.
-You're bad?

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:15.901
You need to pack it in.
-I'm so bad.

00:02:15.901 --> 00:02:19.133
Yeah, because everyone that I've
talked to says I need hobbies.

00:02:19.133 --> 00:02:21.167
So I've been on a mission
for some hobbies.

00:02:21.167 --> 00:02:24.300
-Oh, interesting.
What are you leaning towards?

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:27.868
-I love music. I don't think
I was musically built.

00:02:27.868 --> 00:02:29.434
But I love music.

00:02:29.434 --> 00:02:31.234
-You can build it.

00:02:31.234 --> 00:02:34.067
-Hmm.
-What are you thinking?

00:02:34.067 --> 00:02:35.767
-So, all of my hobbies,
I feel like

00:02:35.767 --> 00:02:39.701
are pretty evidently tied to
when I'm on genius.com too much

00:02:39.701 --> 00:02:42.400
watching producer videos,
explaining like --

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:44.467
In the back of A$AP Ferg's
"Plain Jane"

00:02:44.467 --> 00:02:47.400
is an actual train running
from the subway station.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.334
So, I went from that video
and then downloaded,

00:02:49.334 --> 00:02:51.734
like, a $100 software
to become a producer.

00:02:51.734 --> 00:02:53.534
-Wow.
-But I realized you need

00:02:53.534 --> 00:02:55.801
musical abilities
for the software.

00:02:55.801 --> 00:02:58.167
-You have the software, though.
-I have the software.

00:02:58.167 --> 00:02:59.701
-So you're starting there.

00:02:59.701 --> 00:03:01.434
How about an instrument
or something?

00:03:01.434 --> 00:03:03.767
-Yeah, I'm rocking with
a glockenspiel right now.

00:03:03.767 --> 00:03:05.167
-Now, I don't think

00:03:05.167 --> 00:03:07.667
anyone's ever said that ever
in their life.

00:03:07.667 --> 00:03:10.868
"Rockin' with a glockenspiel."
It rhymes.

00:03:10.868 --> 00:03:13.234
I think it's -- It's like
a xylophone type of thing.

00:03:13.234 --> 00:03:14.834
-Yeah, yeah.
-Wow.

00:03:14.834 --> 00:03:17.167
-I have a glockenspiel
and a dream right now.

00:03:17.167 --> 00:03:18.868
It's just me in my room.

00:03:18.868 --> 00:03:20.701
-Wow. A glockenspiel.

00:03:20.701 --> 00:03:23.033
Yara, of all the things.
-And a talk box.

00:03:23.033 --> 00:03:25.467
Because I had seen that cover
of Stevie Wonder

00:03:25.467 --> 00:03:27.300
doing "Never Can Say Goodbye"
and "Close to You"

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:29.868
on the talk box, and I was like,
"I need one of those."

00:03:29.868 --> 00:03:31.701
-Wait.
Is that one of those, like --

00:03:31.701 --> 00:03:33.367
[ Imitating talk box ]

00:03:33.367 --> 00:03:35.534
-I didn't realize
that you needed an instrument

00:03:35.534 --> 00:03:37.267
to generate sound.

00:03:37.267 --> 00:03:39.801
I thought it just
kind of worked independently.

00:03:39.801 --> 00:03:42.000
-So are you using it
with the glockenspiel?

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:43.801
You're like --
[ Imitating talk box ]

00:03:43.801 --> 00:03:45.334
-I actually --
I have a keyboard

00:03:45.334 --> 00:03:47.767
that I also can't play,
that I connect it to.

00:03:47.767 --> 00:03:49.901
-Will you promise
next time you come on

00:03:49.901 --> 00:03:52.000
we'll bring and play with one
of those three instruments?

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:53.767
Please?
-Yes. Give me some time.

00:03:53.767 --> 00:03:55.767
-Alright.
And we'll work on something.

00:03:55.767 --> 00:03:57.167
-But this would be motivation.
Like, I have a deadline.

00:03:57.167 --> 00:03:58.634
-Alright. Good. Yeah.
A little motivation.

00:03:58.634 --> 00:04:02.834
Do you keep in touch with any
of your students from school?

00:04:02.834 --> 00:04:06.167
-Yeah, I have a lot of wonderful
friends that I made at school.

00:04:06.167 --> 00:04:08.767
-Good. Do they know, like,
kind of what you do?

00:04:08.767 --> 00:04:11.000
-For sure. I would even
bring some of my friends

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:13.300
to events with me, which is,
I think, my favorite part,

00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:14.734
was being able to take, like,

00:04:14.734 --> 00:04:16.067
some of my homies from school
or home.

00:04:16.067 --> 00:04:18.400
-Yeah. Yeah. And go ahead
and take them to a big event.

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:20.868
Like, what, like a...?
-Oh, I mean, one time --

00:04:20.868 --> 00:04:22.334
I mean, one of my friends,
Anishka,

00:04:22.334 --> 00:04:24.267
we went to a Golden Globes
after-party some years ago.

00:04:24.267 --> 00:04:25.701
-Yeah.
-And I was doing

00:04:25.701 --> 00:04:28.834
a really bad job of explaining
what they're about to walk into,

00:04:28.834 --> 00:04:31.334
which is, I guess, the
equivalent of a fever dream.

00:04:31.334 --> 00:04:33.267
All of the people
that you see on TV

00:04:33.267 --> 00:04:36.934
in one place just acting
really normal, and so...

00:04:36.934 --> 00:04:39.033
-Yeah, no, you're not
really ready for that.

00:04:39.033 --> 00:04:40.200
-No.

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:41.667
-So, who did you run into
when you walked in?

00:04:41.667 --> 00:04:44.734
-So, I remember taking her, and
it was just all of the Avengers.

00:04:44.734 --> 00:04:48.200
[ Laughter ]
-It's all of the Avengers.

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:50.534
It was like,
"What is going on right now?!"

00:04:50.534 --> 00:04:52.033
-In one place.
-Wowzers.

00:04:52.033 --> 00:04:53.968
-Yeah.
-That's pretty untoppable.

00:04:53.968 --> 00:04:56.100
-I mean, I think
that's my favorite part,

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:57.868
is, like,
having my friends' reactions.

00:04:57.868 --> 00:05:00.167
And then I love
that everyone's takeaway

00:05:00.167 --> 00:05:01.868
is, like, "Everyone is so nice."

00:05:01.868 --> 00:05:05.200
-Is there any...the Avengers
where your friend was like,

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:06.467
"I've just got to go home"?

00:05:06.467 --> 00:05:07.834
-Oh, I brought one friend,
recently this summer.

00:05:07.834 --> 00:05:10.467
We went to the -- When the
"Renaissance" album dropped,

00:05:10.467 --> 00:05:13.167
got invited to
a "Renaissance" party here.

00:05:13.167 --> 00:05:14.834
-Like a listening party?

00:05:14.834 --> 00:05:16.367
-A listening party.
And I didn't know.

00:05:16.367 --> 00:05:18.400
I couldn't make any promises
to be like,

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:20.667
"Uh, yeah, you're for sure
going to see Beyoncé."

00:05:20.667 --> 00:05:22.534
I feel like
that's out of my lane,

00:05:22.534 --> 00:05:24.701
to make those kind of
commitments to my friends.

00:05:24.701 --> 00:05:27.267
So I just thought, like,
I'd be happy if we were just

00:05:27.267 --> 00:05:29.267
listening to the album
in a space together.

00:05:29.267 --> 00:05:31.133
That'd be cool.
-Yeah, that's fun, yeah.

00:05:31.133 --> 00:05:33.701
Bring your glockenspiel,
and everyone just plays along.

00:05:33.701 --> 00:05:36.334
-I was ready for a jam session.
-Yeah, of course.

00:05:36.334 --> 00:05:40.167
-That was me
playing the glockenspiel.

00:05:40.167 --> 00:05:43.501
But, yeah, we walk in,
and it's real casual.

00:05:43.501 --> 00:05:45.133
I see some family friends

00:05:45.133 --> 00:05:46.801
that have known me
since I was, like, 7.

00:05:46.801 --> 00:05:49.434
And then all of a sudden,
everything just shifts.

00:05:49.434 --> 00:05:52.501
And then in the first 20
minutes, I'm like, "Hi, Beyoncé.

00:05:52.501 --> 00:05:55.767
This is my friend from
Africana philosophy class."

00:05:55.767 --> 00:05:57.901
-So Beyoncé is there.
-She's there.

00:05:57.901 --> 00:05:59.534
-And what is your friend doing?

00:05:59.534 --> 00:06:02.000
-I mean, I think we were --
-She was cool?

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:03.667
-Yeah, 'cause there are certain
people -- it doesn't matter

00:06:03.667 --> 00:06:04.934
how often you meet them --

00:06:04.934 --> 00:06:06.701
just always in awe
of their presence.

00:06:06.701 --> 00:06:08.133
-Beyoncé, of course.
-Yeah.

00:06:08.133 --> 00:06:09.801
-Are you kidding me?
-But again, her takeaway

00:06:09.801 --> 00:06:11.701
was, like, they're all
like aunties and uncles.

00:06:11.701 --> 00:06:13.267
Like, everyone
was checking in on me.

00:06:13.267 --> 00:06:15.234
I think my favorite thing is,
if I go to a party,

00:06:15.234 --> 00:06:17.901
people will text my mom like,
"You know she's going to this?"

00:06:17.901 --> 00:06:20.200
-Yeah. Exactly right.

00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:21.834
-And she's like, "Yeah.
I sent her off there."

00:06:21.834 --> 00:06:23.334
-I love that.
-Yeah.

00:06:23.334 --> 00:06:25.501
-Can we talk about your movie
that you're going to be in?

00:06:25.501 --> 00:06:26.801
-Oh!
-Or we're not allowed to?

00:06:26.801 --> 00:06:28.734
-Yeah, let's do it.
-I mean, this is pretty giant.

00:06:28.734 --> 00:06:30.901
You play Tinkerbell.
-Yeah.

00:06:30.901 --> 00:06:32.601
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:32.601 --> 00:06:34.234
-I didn't know
if we can talk about it.

00:06:34.234 --> 00:06:35.467
-No, we can.
-We can?

00:06:35.467 --> 00:06:37.033
-Yeah, we can talk about it.
-Okay, okay, okay.

00:06:37.033 --> 00:06:38.300
I don't know
when it's coming out.

00:06:38.300 --> 00:06:39.834
I just know Disney's making
"Peter Pan," right?

00:06:39.834 --> 00:06:43.167
-Mm-hmm. "Peter Pan &amp; Wendy."
-And you play Tinkerbell.

00:06:43.167 --> 00:06:45.934
-Yes, I play quite literally
a small part in the movie.

00:06:45.934 --> 00:06:47.100
[ Laughter ]

00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:48.367
-Literally. That big. Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:06:48.367 --> 00:06:50.300
-What was that like?
-It was incredible.

00:06:50.300 --> 00:06:53.100
And I thought I was being punked
the whole time

00:06:53.100 --> 00:06:55.501
because, you know --

00:06:55.501 --> 00:06:57.667
I know I'm being part of
"Peter Pan &amp; Wendy."

00:06:57.667 --> 00:06:59.200
And you see,
with "Avatar" coming out,

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:01.067
what behind the scenes
of all these

00:07:01.067 --> 00:07:02.667
CGI motion-capture movies
look like.

00:07:02.667 --> 00:07:05.133
So I was ready for the harnesses

00:07:05.133 --> 00:07:07.367
and the suits
with the dots on it.

00:07:07.367 --> 00:07:08.767
-Yeah.

00:07:08.767 --> 00:07:11.601
-They were on incredible sets
in, like, Vancouver,

00:07:11.601 --> 00:07:15.667
and I was in a garage in Burbank
the whole time.

00:07:15.667 --> 00:07:17.801
Motion capture --
-Hanging from, like, a rope?

00:07:17.801 --> 00:07:19.267
And you're like,
"Hi, everyone. I'm Tinkerbell!"

00:07:19.267 --> 00:07:21.234
-I wish I could even say
there was a rope involved.

00:07:21.234 --> 00:07:22.634
-There was no rope involved?
-No.

00:07:22.634 --> 00:07:24.033
-You had to pretend
you're flying?

00:07:24.033 --> 00:07:25.501
-Yeah, because they realize --

00:07:25.501 --> 00:07:26.934
I mean, the -- the tech
is so advanced.

00:07:26.934 --> 00:07:29.067
And, you know, to
an untrained eye, which I am,

00:07:29.067 --> 00:07:31.033
it looks like GoPros
on some grip stands,

00:07:31.033 --> 00:07:33.601
like, holding things together.
-It's the future.

00:07:33.601 --> 00:07:36.434
-And I had my castmates
on little poles,

00:07:36.434 --> 00:07:39.400
like, pictures of their face
on little stands around me

00:07:39.400 --> 00:07:40.701
for reference.

00:07:40.701 --> 00:07:42.701
-So, you just acted with
a bunch of -- Yeah, just --

00:07:42.701 --> 00:07:45.033
-Sometimes, when I needed
to act opposite somebody,

00:07:45.033 --> 00:07:49.567
they had a life-size Kermit the
Frog doll for me to work with.

00:07:49.567 --> 00:07:51.133
-Because he's green?
-Yeah.

00:07:51.133 --> 00:07:54.133
-Like the green screen will --
All they see is his eyes.

00:07:54.133 --> 00:07:55.901
-Yeah.
-That's so funny.

00:07:55.901 --> 00:07:59.267
Can we talk "Grown-ish"?
This is part two of season five.

00:07:59.267 --> 00:08:00.968
-Yes.

00:08:00.968 --> 00:08:02.767
-This started today.
Catch us up.

00:08:02.767 --> 00:08:04.234
What do we need to know?

00:08:04.234 --> 00:08:06.033
-What do we need to know?
-What's happening this season?

00:08:06.033 --> 00:08:07.834
-Well, I mean,
it starts off, of course,

00:08:07.834 --> 00:08:10.767
with me passing the baton
to my TV brother, Marcus.

00:08:10.767 --> 00:08:12.367
-That's awesome
that, by the way, he joined.

00:08:12.367 --> 00:08:13.534
-Oh, I was so excited.

00:08:13.534 --> 00:08:15.300
I've been pitching this
since season one.

00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:17.267
So when they told me season five
it was happening,

00:08:17.267 --> 00:08:18.801
I was over the moon.

00:08:18.801 --> 00:08:20.801
So, yeah, it's finally happened.

00:08:20.801 --> 00:08:23.767
Junior is at Cal I,
and Zoe is now a graduated

00:08:23.767 --> 00:08:27.267
big sister that comes back
for big-sister things.

00:08:27.267 --> 00:08:29.834
But she's on her way,
I think, to Tokyo,

00:08:29.834 --> 00:08:33.300
when we catch up with
the second half of season five.

00:08:33.300 --> 00:08:35.767
And there's a little meet-cute
with her guy, Aaron.

00:08:35.767 --> 00:08:37.200
-Oh.
-Yeah.

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:38.968
-Ooh. That's what
I'm talking about.

00:08:38.968 --> 00:08:41.200
Yeah, why not? Of course,
Kenya Barris, we love.

00:08:41.200 --> 00:08:43.334
He's coming on -- I think
he's coming on this week.

00:08:43.334 --> 00:08:45.300
-Oh, for "You People."
I can't wait to watch it.

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:47.501
-Oh, yeah. He's brilliant.
I want to show a clip.

00:08:47.501 --> 00:08:49.167
Here's Yara Shahidi
in "Grown-ish."

00:08:49.167 --> 00:08:50.367
Take a look at this.

00:08:50.367 --> 00:08:51.601
-How long is the delay?

00:08:51.601 --> 00:08:54.601
I'm connecting in Hawaii
to Tokyo.

00:08:54.601 --> 00:08:55.901
-Hold on. Let me check.

00:08:55.901 --> 00:08:57.300
-I'm just trying
to get to Seattle,

00:08:57.300 --> 00:08:59.634
and I'm three quarters
of the way there.

00:08:59.634 --> 00:09:02.267
It's your fault, ma'am. Is there
a manager I can talk to?

00:09:02.267 --> 00:09:04.334
I know you got the juice.
-Air of superiority.

00:09:04.334 --> 00:09:07.934
Defiant statements.
Sexy-as-hell baritone.

00:09:07.934 --> 00:09:09.234
It's not possible.

00:09:09.234 --> 00:09:10.667
-She's not saying
anything, guys.

00:09:10.667 --> 00:09:12.000
Might as well pack your bags...
-Could it be?

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:15.167
-...and go home because
the flights are delayed!

00:09:15.167 --> 00:09:18.167
-[ Gasps ]

00:09:18.167 --> 00:09:20.601
Hahhh!

00:09:20.601 --> 00:09:22.434
Aaron!
-What?!

00:09:23.934 --> 00:09:26.634
-Hi!
-You've got to be kidding me.

00:09:26.634 --> 00:09:29.434
[ Chuckles ]

00:09:29.434 --> 00:09:31.567
-Now, that is exactly
what you want.

00:09:31.567 --> 00:09:33.400
Yara Shahidi, everyone.

00:09:33.400 --> 00:09:37.167
"Grown-ish" airs Wednesdays
at 10:30 p.m. on Freeform

00:09:37.167 --> 00:09:39.300
and is available the next day
on Hulu.

